        <div id="1000-vs-ultra-shield" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Is Better, Black Jack 1000 or Latex-ite Ultra Shield?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Latex-ite Ultra Shield is the better choice for most homeowners, and Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 is the better choice for a tired driveway that needs filling as much as sealing. Both are their brands' top retail tier, both are water-based asphalt emulsions in 4.75-gallon pails, and both claim ten years. The differences are in the fine print: spread per pail, cure temperature and what each is built to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black Jack's data sheet calls Drive-Maxx 1000 its best-quality rubberized blacktop filler and sealer. It is latex-polymer enhanced, carries sand particles to fill small cracks, and is described as urethane enhanced for longest life and as a heavy vehicle formula. That recipe builds a thick, dark film, which is why the label coverage is a modest 350 square feet per coat on smooth asphalt and 300 on rough or worn asphalt. You are paying for solids that stay on the surface, which is exactly what a gray, stony driveway needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Latex-ite describes Ultra Shield as a filler sealer with a flexible polymer system, an EZ-Gel formula, UV protection and a non-skid finish, and it is labeled low VOC, coal tar free and PAH compliant. Its product page lists 400 to 500 square feet per pail per coat on smooth or previously sealed asphalt and 300 to 400 on rough, porous or never-sealed surfaces. On a driveway that was sealed a few years ago, that can mean one fewer pail per coat than the Black Jack flagship. Latex-ite also says its ThermaSeal technology lets the coat cure down to 40°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the decision is mostly about the surface. If you can see the stones and the fine material between them has washed out, a heavier pail like Drive-Maxx 1000 lays down more body per square foot, and that is where it earns its higher price. If the surface is still tight and black underneath the fade, Ultra Shield's wider spread saves money and its cooler cure rating gives you more days on the calendar. Our <a href="/latex-ite-driveway-sealer-review/">Latex-ite driveway sealer review</a> goes deeper on the Latex-ite side, and the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> place both pails against the rest of the market.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the pail to the texture, not the year on the label.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rough, open asphalt wants the heavier fill of Drive-Maxx 1000. Smooth, previously sealed asphalt gets more square feet per dollar from Ultra Shield. Both labels require clean, dry pavement and two thin coats to deliver what they promise.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="product-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Are the Black Jack Drive-Maxx and Latex-ite Product Tiers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Black Jack sells five Drive-Maxx tiers and Latex-ite sells four blacktop sealers, according to the two makers' own product pages checked on November 18, 2026. Black Jack's numbering is simple: the number divided by 100 is the year claim printed on the product page, from Drive-Maxx 200 (2 year) up to Drive-Maxx 1000 (10 year). Latex-ite names its tiers instead, and its year figures are stated as warranties on the product pages.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Black Jack (year claim, maker list price)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Latex-ite (warranty, notes)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Entry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drive-Maxx 200: 2 year, $19.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dura Sand Mix: 2-year warranty, rubberized, traction sand</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Step up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drive-Maxx 300: 3 year, $20.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drive-Maxx 500: 5 year, $29.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Airport Grade+: 5-year warranty, polymer fortified</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drive-Maxx 700: 7 year, $36.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flagship</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drive-Maxx 1000: 10 year, $44.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultra Shield: 10-year warranty, cures to 40°F</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Different chemistry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic Grade: acrylic, roller-applied, 3.4 gal</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Latex-ite Airport Grade is the name many shoppers still search for, and the current version on the maker's site is Airport Grade+, a polymer-fortified filler sealer with a 5-year warranty and the same 400-500 and 300-400 square foot coverage ranges as Ultra Shield. A Latex-ite Pro-Grade sealer does not appear in the maker's current blacktop lineup, and the Optimum Pro resurfacer is listed as discontinued by at least one retailer. If you find either on a shelf, read the pail's own label rather than assuming it matches a current product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black Jack's lineup has also been renamed: product pages for older Drive-Kote, Drive-Seal and New Black pails now point to Drive-Maxx numbers, so store listings still mix the names. For either brand, buy by tier, read the coverage and year claim on the actual pail, and buy every pail for one job from the same tier and batch code, which Black Jack's data sheet recommends to avoid color shading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Latex-ite Acrylic Grade is the odd one out: an acrylic in a 3.4-gallon pail that the maker says must be rolled on, with car traffic claimed in four hours. If you are weighing chemistries rather than brands, our <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based vs water-based driveway sealer</a> comparison explains where each family fits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drive-maxx-500" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Black Jack Drive-Maxx 500 Review: Is the Mid-Tier Pail Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Black Jack Drive-Maxx 500 is a reasonable mid-tier pail for a sound driveway sealed on a regular schedule, but it has a narrower weather window than the flagship. Black Jack lists it as a 5-year blacktop filler and sealer at $29.99, with an E-Z stir, latex-enhanced formula. Its data sheet gives the same coverage as Drive-Maxx 1000, 350 square feet per coat on smooth asphalt and 300 on rough or worn asphalt, so you do not save pails by stepping down, only dollars per pail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important difference is temperature. The Drive-Maxx 500 data sheet says air temperature must be 55°F and rising and below 90°F during application, with no rain or overnight lows below 50°F expected within 36 hours. Drive-Maxx 1000 is rated to 45°F. In practice that means the 500 is a summer pail, and a September or early-spring job in the northern states is a better fit for the flagship or for Latex-ite Ultra Shield. The 500 also lists slower foot traffic, about 2 to 4 hours, with 24 to 48 hours before vehicles.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 5-year figure deserves a careful read. The data sheet's warranty language covers the product against defects for five years from the date of purchase; it is not a promise that one application will look fresh for five years on your driveway. Owners commonly report that mid-tier emulsions need a refresh every two to three years on a driveway with daily traffic, with turning areas and shaded sections wearing first. Plan your budget on that cycle and treat any extra year as a bonus.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Drive-Maxx 500 for a smooth driveway in decent shape, sealed in midsummer, on a two-to-three-year cycle. Skip it for cool-weather jobs, for rough, stony asphalt that wants the heavier 700 or 1000, or if Latex-ite Airport Grade+ costs about the same at your store, since its label lists wider coverage on smooth asphalt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Covers More per Pail, and How Long Does Each Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Latex-ite covers more per pail on paper, and neither brand's year number tells you how long a coat will last on your driveway. Latex-ite lists 400 to 500 square feet per 4.75-gallon pail per coat on smooth or previously sealed asphalt for both Ultra Shield and Airport Grade+, and 300 to 400 on rough, porous or never-sealed asphalt. Black Jack's data sheets list 350 square feet on smooth and 300 on rough or worn asphalt for Drive-Maxx 500 and 1000. Both makers add that coverage depends on the condition of the existing surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the numbers for a typical 600 square foot single-car driveway that needs two coats, or 1,200 square feet of spreading. On rough asphalt, Black Jack's 300-square-foot figure works out to four pails, and Latex-ite's 300 to 400 range works out to three or four. On smooth, previously sealed asphalt, Black Jack still needs about four pails, while Latex-ite's 400 to 500 range needs three. Very thirsty, oxidized driveways often drink more than any label suggests, so buy one spare pail you can return unopened; our sibling pages plan on 250 to 350 square feet a pail for older, porous blacktop for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Pails for a 600 sq ft drive, two coats (label figures)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Black Jack Drive-Maxx, smooth or rough</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Latex-ite Ultra Shield, smooth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3-4</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Latex-ite Ultra Shield, rough</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Durability is where the marketing gets ahead of the driveway. Black Jack prints 2, 3, 5, 7 and 10 years on its tiers, and Latex-ite states 2-year, 5-year and 10-year warranties. These are tier names and warranty periods, and both makers attach conditions: Latex-ite says its warranty is honored only when any repair products used were Latex-ite products and voids it for roller application, and Black Jack's data sheet makes two thin coats a requirement and points to its own warranty terms. Read the warranty terms on the maker's site before you count on one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owners commonly report about two to three years from a well-applied two-coat job with either brand, longer in full sun and shorter in shade or where a plow scrapes, which matches our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt sealer rankings</a>. Premium tiers tend to hold color and resist scuffs longer, but no pail in this aisle turns a two-year job into a ten-year one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-times" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Dries Faster and Works in Cooler Weather?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two flagships dry about equally fast, and Latex-ite Ultra Shield has the lower cold limit. Black Jack says Drive-Maxx 1000 is ready for light foot traffic in about one hour under good drying conditions, which it defines as temperatures above 75°F, clear skies and humidity below 35 percent, and that vehicles must wait a minimum of 24 hours; its application notes give 24 to 48 hours. Latex-ite says Ultra Shield is foot-traffic ready in one hour. Its product page does not state a vehicle time, so allow the same day or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold-weather ratings differ more. Latex-ite rates Ultra Shield to cure down to 40°F. Black Jack rates Drive-Maxx 1000 for application from 45°F to 99°F and says not to apply if rain or overnight lows below 45°F are expected within 36 hours. The mid tiers are stricter: Latex-ite asks for at least 55°F at night for Airport Grade+ and warns against sealing in early spring or late fall, and Black Jack wants 55°F and rising for Drive-Maxx 500 with overnight lows above 50°F.
          </p>

