          <p>
            A low rating is a minimum, not a target. Every emulsion cures faster and harder in warm sun, so a 40°F rating is most useful for squeezing in a job in late September, not for planning one in November. Black Jack also asks you to apply onto a slightly damp, not wet, surface after rinsing and to put the second coat on perpendicular to the first once the first has cured, roughly two to four hours later. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> covers the full sequence from cleaning to the second coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-where-sold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Do Black Jack and Latex-ite Cost, and Where Are They Sold?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Black Jack publishes list prices from $19.99 for Drive-Maxx 200 to $44.99 for Drive-Maxx 1000 on its own site, checked November 18, 2026. Latex-ite does not publish prices on its product pages, so its pails are priced by each retailer. In stores, the two brands usually sit in the same price bands tier for tier: entry pails near the bottom of the aisle, mid tiers in the middle and the 10-year flagships at the top. Shelf prices move through the season and between regions, so compare on the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare cost per square foot, not per pail. A pail that costs a few dollars more but spreads 450 square feet instead of 350 is the cheaper job. On rough asphalt the gap narrows, because both brands drop to about 300 square feet per coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you can buy them matters, because you want every pail from one store and one batch. Black Jack Drive-Maxx pails are listed at Home Depot, Ace Hardware, Tractor Supply and Canadian Tire, among others. Latex-ite pails are listed at Home Depot, Ace Hardware, Do it Best stores and Tractor Supply, and Henry sells Airport Grade in Canada. Stock is seasonal: shelves fill in spring and thin out by early fall. Our <a href="/driveway-sealer-home-depot/">Home Depot driveway sealer guide</a> lists what that chain typically carries and how many pails common driveway sizes need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reviews-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Consumer Reports and Owner Reviews Say About Black Jack and Latex-ite?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not verify a current Consumer Reports result for these exact pails, so this page does not quote one. Consumer Reports runs its own lab evaluations and publishes ratings to its members; any ranking it gives Black Jack or Latex-ite is its work, not ours, and its scores may cover older formulas than the ones now on shelves. If you subscribe, check the date of the rating and the exact product name, since both brands have renamed tiers several times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owner reviews on retailer sites follow the same pattern for both brands. The common praise is appearance: a deep, even black that makes an old driveway look new for the first season, with Black Jack's flagship often singled out for its dark finish. The common complaints are coverage below the label on old, porous asphalt, the effort of stirring a heavy pail, and peeling where the surface was dirty, damp or sealed too thick. Those show up under both logos, which says more about prep than brand. Comparisons that crown a clear winner often quietly pit one brand's entry pail against the other's flagship, so check the tiers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="epoxy-best-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Either One an Epoxy Asphalt Sealer, and What Is the Best Brand of Driveway Sealer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Neither Black Jack nor Latex-ite sells an epoxy driveway sealer; both make water-based asphalt emulsions modified with latex, rubber or polymer, plus one acrylic pail from Latex-ite. The best-known epoxy-branded option in the same aisle is Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Blacktop, which is a different product family. Shoppers searching for epoxy asphalt sealer brands usually want a harder, more chemical-resistant finish, and on a home driveway the practical options are a premium polymer emulsion, an acrylic, or a contractor-applied sealcoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no single best brand of driveway sealer, but there is a best tier for each driveway. For most homeowners with sound asphalt, a polymer-modified filler sealer from a major brand, applied in two thin coats in warm weather, is the right buy. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> put Latex-ite Airport Grade first for most drives and Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 as the premium-finish pick, and this comparison agrees: Latex-ite for value and cooler weather, Black Jack for a heavy coat on worn asphalt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Safety is similar for both. Both are water-based and clean up with soap and water while wet, and Latex-ite labels its blacktop sealers coal tar free and PAH compliant. Black Jack's data sheet still calls for protective clothing and eyewear because the product can irritate skin, and asks you to transport pails upright in the cargo area, never on a passenger seat. Wear gloves and safety glasses, keep children and pets off the drive until it cures, and never pour leftover sealer into a storm drain. If you are offered a solvent-based or coal tar sealer instead, read our <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil vs water-based comparison</a> first, since those need more ventilation and are banned in some states and cities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes With Black Jack and Latex-ite Sealers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing pail prices instead of cost per square foot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Black Jack pail lists 350 square feet per coat on smooth asphalt and a Latex-ite Ultra Shield pail lists 400 to 500. The cheaper pail can be the more expensive driveway. Divide the price by the label coverage for your surface before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the year on the pail as a promise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 5, 7 and 10 on these pails are tier names and warranty periods with conditions attached. Owners commonly report two to three years between coats on a busy driveway. Budget on that cycle and treat anything longer as a bonus.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing brands or tiers on one job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Black Jack's data sheet says not to blend its sealer with other driveway products and to match batch codes across pails. Buy every pail for one job from the same brand, tier and batch so the finish does not shade from one section to the next.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using another brand's crack filler and expecting the warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Latex-ite says its warranty is honored only if any repair products used were Latex-ite products, and Black Jack points owners to its own crack fillers. If the warranty matters to you, fill cracks with the same brand as the sealer and keep the receipts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a mid-tier pail in cool weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drive-Maxx 500 and Airport Grade+ both want overnight temperatures in the 50s. Applying them in a cold snap leaves a soft coat that tracks and wears early. In shoulder seasons, use a flagship rated to 40-45°F or wait for warmer nights.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does Latex-ite compare to Black Jack sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tier for tier, they are close. Both sell water-based asphalt emulsions in 4.75-gallon pails with 2-, 5- and 10-year tiers. Latex-ite's labels list more coverage on smooth asphalt, 400 to 500 square feet per pail against 350, and its Ultra Shield cures down to 40°F. Black Jack's Drive-Maxx 1000 is a heavier rubberized, urethane-enhanced pail suited to rough, worn surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000 better than Latex-ite Ultra Shield?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for every driveway. Drive-Maxx 1000 is the better pick on a gray, stony surface that needs filling, because its heavy body lays down more material per square foot. Ultra Shield is the better pick on smooth or previously sealed asphalt, where its wider label coverage saves a pail per coat, and in cool weather, since it is rated to cure at 40°F.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Has Consumer Reports rated Black Jack vs Latex-ite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Consumer Reports publishes its own ratings to members, and we could not verify a current result for these exact pails, so we do not quote one. If you check, confirm the rating date and the exact tier name, because both brands have renamed products and an older rating may cover a formula that is no longer sold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Black Jack Drive-Maxx 500 worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a fair mid-tier pail for a smooth driveway sealed in midsummer. Black Jack lists it at $29.99 with a 5-year claim and the same 350 and 300 square foot coverage as the flagship. Its data sheet requires 55°F and rising and overnight lows above 50°F, so it is a poor fit for spring or fall jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put Latex-ite over an old Black Jack coat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, once the old coat is fully cured, clean and well bonded, because both are water-based asphalt emulsions. Do not mix the two in a pail. Pressure wash, degrease oil spots, let the surface dry, and follow the new pail's label. Peeling or flaking old sealer should be scraped and swept off first, or the new coat will fail with it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply Black Jack or Latex-ite with a roller?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a squeegee for both flagship lines. Black Jack says a squeegee must be used to spread the product at the right rate, and Latex-ite says a roller is the wrong applicator for Ultra Shield and voids the warranty. The exception is Latex-ite Acrylic Grade, which the maker says must be rolled on.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Black Jack and Latex-ite coal tar sealers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The current lines covered here are water-based asphalt emulsions, not coal tar. Latex-ite labels Ultra Shield, Airport Grade+ and Dura Sand Mix coal tar free and PAH compliant. Coal tar sealers are banned in several states and cities. Check the label on the exact pail you buy, and wear gloves and eye protection either way.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

